infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title When HTTP/3 wins and when HTTP/2 is the better call
  desc The senior rule: HTTP/3 at the user-facing edge, HTTP/2 for internal east-west
  compares
    - label HTTP/3 over QUIC
      children
        - label Per-packet loss tracking
          desc One lost packet stalls only the streams it carried — stream isolation holds
        - label Wins on lossy paths
          desc Cellular 0.5-2%, satellite 2-5% loss; long-RTT mobile gains the most
        - label Tunable congestion control
          desc Runs in userspace — swap algorithm per connection, no kernel change
        - label Blocked by UDP firewalls
          desc 5-15% of corporate paths drop UDP/443, forcing silent h2 fallback
    - label HTTP/2 over TCP
      children
        - label In-order byte stream
          desc One lost segment stalls all 50 streams until the retransmit completes
        - label Wins on reliable links
          desc Datacentre east-west on 10 GbE under 0.1% loss — HOL blocking invisible
        - label Kernel-offloaded crypto
          desc TCP+TLS in the kernel — less CPU than QUIC's userspace stack
        - label Traverses every middlebox
          desc Plain TCP/443 — no UDP-block risk, no fallback round-trip to pay
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
